(UPDATING ) Tj
/F00 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 102.0 509.6 Tm
0.2480 Tw
(Now that you know how to read, write and create MARC data, you have the tools you need to update or) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 497.6 Tm
0.0507 Tw
(edit exiting MARC data. Updating MARC data is a common task for library catalogers. Sometimes there) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 485.6 Tm
1.9583 Tw
(are huge amounts of records that need to be touched up... and while the touch ups are very detail) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 473.6 Tm
1.5720 Tw
(oriented, they are also highly repetitive. Luckily, computers are tireless, and not very prone to error) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 461.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(\(assuming the programmer isn`t\). ) Tj
/F00 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 102.0 449.6 Tm
0.0200 Tw
(When libraries receive large batches of MARC records for electronic text collections such as NetLibrary,) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 437.6 Tm
0.7580 Tw
(Making of America, or microfiche sets like Early American Imprints, the records are often loaded into) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 425.6 Tm
0.4371 Tw
(an online system and then the system is used to update the records. Unfortunately, not all these systems) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 413.6 Tm
0.5394 Tw
(are created equal, and catalogers have to spend a great deal of time touching up each individual record.) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 401.6 Tm
0.4917 Tw
(An alternative would be to process the records prior to import and then, once in the system, the records) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 389.6 Tm
0.8778 Tw
(would not need editing. This scenario would save a great deal of time for the cataloger who would be) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 377.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(liberated to spend their time doing original cataloging... which computers are notably bad at! ) Tj
/F21 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 72.0 359.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(Adding a field ) Tj
/F00 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 102.0 347.6 Tm
1.5100 Tw
(Imagine a batch of records in `file.dat' that you`d like to add local notes \(590\) to, then saving your) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 335.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(changes: ) Tj
/F10 10 Tf
1 0 0 1 102.0 323.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   1   ## Example U1 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 311.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   2 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 299.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   3   ## create our MARC::Batch object. ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 287.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   4   use MARC::Batch; ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 275.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   5   my $batch = MARC::Batch->new\('USMARC','file.dat'\); ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 263.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   6 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 251.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   7   ## open a file handle to write to. ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 239.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   8   open\(OUT,'>new.dat'\) or die $!; ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 227.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(   9 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 215.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  10   ## read each record, modify, then print. ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 203.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  11   while \( my $record = $batch->next\(\) \) \{ ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 191.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  12 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 179.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  13       ## add a 590 field. ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 167.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  14       $record->append_fields\( ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 155.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  15          MARC::Field->new\('590','','',a=>'Access provided by Enron.'\) ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 143.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  16       \); ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 131.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
(  17 ) Tj
1 0 0 1 102.0 119.6 Tm
0.0000 Tw
